 because of  the  lymph of the small amount of material  that one really gets from biopsies  they grow out quite fast  usually the the average  uh the medium the average splitting  ratio is one to four  um  every  11 days  and this is the typical histology  again these are mostly clusters of cells  with thick epithelium and varied lumens  they have moderate to  high nuclear pleueomorphism they have  some mitotic events and also they're all  ck19 positive  our next set were lung organoids and we  have actually published a paper on this  they have been challenging to grow to  say the least we have um been growing  them from not for non-small cell  carcinoma patients and also from small  cell carcinoma patients  success rate varies from 10 to 50  percent our best success rate is growing  them from small cell lung cancer tissues  and from non-small cell  xenograft tissues  the biggest issues in growing these from  the primary resected  tissues is the normal outgrowth um that  has been documented in literature  uh and that definitely has been uh has  been a  has been one of the  issues um in the core  to generate  these models  but the passage rate uh  the the average fast and splitting ratio  is one to three and usually every every  10 to 10 days to 14 days  and here is also also we have a doubling  rate for these models generally the  doubling rate is about 58 hours  histology varies with the diagnosis so  adenocarcinoma  squamous uh small cell  carcinoma they're all have  sort of different uh histologies  um and  for  adenocarcinoma organoid models are  generally ttf1 positive where squamous  shows positivity for p63  and these are some of the representative  histologies  another set of models  esophageal organoids are from esophageal  adenocarcinoma we grown them from both  reflected tissues and biopsy tissues the  success rate is from 50 to 70  these models um grow pretty fast one to  four splitting ratio every 11 days and  the doubling rate is about 54.  average average doubling rate is about  50 for  54 hours  this is what some of these organized  models look like they're clusters of  cells  and they have varied lumens  so now the last two tumor types have  been very challenging so one of them was  ovarian  while majority of our  ovarian tissues will generate or  initiate organoids not many of them can  actually be biobanked because they sh  they grow only for a short period of  time we sort of indicate that the media  is not or the technology is you know not  adequate to grow these models  but the average and also they grow a bit  slower than the rest so the average  splitting ratio is about one to three  and they're they're  generally passaged every two to three  weeks  and the doubling rate um is also about  seven average 72 hours  so our highest success rate would  probably be from ascites fluid and of  course our lowest success rate would be  from the primary sector cases  biopsies are  super difficult we haven't really  been able to grow too many um  long-term organoid models from those  and the finally breast organoid models  again we have processed many tissues and  you use many different types of medias  um and  however  that that can be bio-banked  most of these organoids so the success  rate is low is 10 to 30 percent and most  of the ones that we actually made are  either high grade or triple negative  so the low grade are generally very  difficult to make  and number one issue  again you know if i was to show you  initiation rates you know it would  probably be anywhere between 90 to 100  most of these will grow something  however the major issue here is the  contamination of normal cells  and of the normal organoids  um so this is an important thing to  address  moving forward  um you know because it's important to  incorporate appropriate qcs when using  these models  in early  passages because if you want to use them  for drug screens you got to make sure  that whatever you're growing  is not of normal origin