uite interesting um so here is an  example of a breast organoid that's  derived from xenograft tissue so as you  can see here there seem to be two  populations of organoids that are  apparent here  small organoids and really large dark  organoids so these large dark structures  are actually mouth cells  and if you're not properly handling them  they will take over the entire cultures  and you might end up working with mouth  cells without without knowing  so we have incorporated a qc step when  processing xenograft tissue  and so we use h2k antibody depletion so  this way we deplete mouth cells  after dissociation so in hoping hoping  to enrich for uh the tumor cells um that  are actually being plated for major for  generation  while this has improved our success rate  there are still many cultures that have  a large proportion of mouth organized  that just like to grow  and so if working with such cultures one  should periodically perform flow  analysis to confirm  that you are indeed working with human  tumor organoids and not mass  so again if using these four drug  screens this qc step uh seems to be  necessary  so these are the lessons that we have  learned  basically we  we need to establish the clear criteria  for organization establishment  definitions  what is initiation what are short-term  what are long-term cultures  we try to address um the normal  outgrowths and and perform proper qc  to identify it um  insufficient starting material is always  a problem if you don't have enough tumor  cells it's very difficult to initiate  these organoids  mouse contamination should be addressed  and you know continuously improve the  organ with technology whether or not  it's by  using multiple medias consider  because we need to consider  heterogeneity of these tissues  using alternative to matrigel and also  find ways to eliminate the normal  outgrowth  so  let's now look at the ways we utilize  these models for different projects and  we work with many principal  